  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 220 PM MDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nAfter a warm and largely dry weekend, the weather will turn more \nunsettled once again as we head into the early part of next week. We \nwill have an interesting synoptic setup across the area, with the \ntypical upper level ridge over the southern Plains, combined with an \napproaching Pacific trough. The wild-card factor here will be the \npotential for an infusion of an exceptionally moist airmass ahead of \nand associated with Hurricane Polo as it moves into Baja California \nearly next week. Unfortunately for our area which remains in drought \nconditions, these factors do not look likely to align and produce \nheavy rainfall for our area. Instead, the Pacific trough is trending \ntowards a split trough scenario. The northern branch will move \nthrough on Monday, leaving behind a closed low in the southwest, \nwhich eventually absorbs the remnants of Hurricane Polo. Moisture \nwill be abundant by Monday, with PW values around the 90th to 99th \npercentile of climatology, but forcing and instability look \nunimpressive at this time as the core of the northern branch of the \nsplit trough passes to our north. Still, we should have enough to at \nleast support scattered showers and possibly a few thunderstorms. \nLook for increased cloud cover and slightly cooler temperatures as a \nresult, though highs should still be a touch above seasonal \naverages. A cool front with the northern branch system will knock \ntemperatures down by about 5-10 degrees for Tuesday. A chance for \nshowers remains in the forecast, but the most likely scenario at \nthis time leaves the closed low, and thus the potential for \nwidespread heavy rainfall, mainly to our south. \n\nHeading into the middle of the week, the most likely scenario \nsupported by about 80+% of ensemble members features seasonable \ntemperatures and dry conditions as the upper level closed low spins \nto our south. Cloud cover may spill into our southern zones \nassociated with the system to the south. While the probability of a \ndifferent scenario is low, it is still worth a mention. In about 20% \nof ensemble members (and the AI-GFS), the closed low over the \nsouthwest lifts more to the north instead of the east, which would \nbring abundant wrap-around moisture into our High Plains zones \nWednesday into early Thursday. Regardless of the precipitation \npotential, there is also a chance for some strong winds behind this \nsystem. The official forecast reflects the former scenario, which is \nfar more likely at this time, but we can't quite rule out the latter \njust yet. After the closed low moves out, expect another warm-up \nbeginning Thursday, and peaking on Friday.",
